.TH JENT_ENTROPY_COLLECTOR_ALLOC 3 2026-09-30 "jitterentropy x.y.z" "Library Functions Manual"
.\" groff re-enables hyphenation per paragraph unless HY is 0.
.nr HY 0
.nh
.SH NAME
jent_entropy_collector_alloc, jent_entropy_collector_free \- allocate and free a CPU Jitter RNG entropy collector
.SH LIBRARY
Jitter RNG library
.RI ( libjitterentropy ", " \-ljitterentropy ).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <jitterentropy.h>
.sp
.BI "struct rand_data *jent_entropy_collector_alloc(unsigned int " osr ",
.BI "                                               unsigned int " flags );
.sp
.BI "void jent_entropy_collector_free(struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
allocates a CPU Jitter entropy collector instance and returns the handle
to the caller. If the allocation fails, including memory
constraints, the call returns
.IR NULL .
The function requires two arguments, the oversampling rate
.IR osr
and a set of flags with
.IR flags .
The
.IR osr
value defines the amount of oversampling performed by the entropy
collector. Usually, a caller wants to provide the value 0 here to
apply the default oversampling. The call raises any value below 3 to 3 automatically, and so does
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex (3).
A value above 20 is refused with a NULL return instead.
These bounds are
.B JENT_MIN_OSR
and
.B JENT_MAX_OSR
of
.IR jitterentropy.h ;
.BR jent_flags_invalid ()
there tells the flags every allocation refuses.
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_free ()
zeroizes and frees the given CPU Jitter entropy collector instance.
.SS Flags
The
.IR flags
value is either zero or one or more of the following flags.
.TP
.B JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS
If the system is constrained with memory, this flag
disables the allocation of that memory and therefore memory accesses. But
that also implies that the entropy collection process only relies on the
complexity of the CPU. Note, if somebody knows all details of that CPU
complexity, that person may potentially reduce the entropy delivered by the CPU
complexity. If that person can push the generated entropy below a threshold,
the CPU Jitter random number generator starts overestimating entropy from the
noise source. Thus, disabling memory accesses and relying only on the CPU
complexity should only be done if you really know what you are doing.
.TP
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
This flag can be used to force the Jitter RNG to use the internal
high-resolution timer instead of using the hardware time stamp. Without it,
an allocation uses the hardware timer with precedence if a startup self test
passed on it, and falls back to the internal timer for that instance alone
otherwise. The startup self test of
.BR jent_entropy_init (3)
and
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex (3)
measures the common divisor of the time deltas once per timer, which every
instance on that timer uses. If the internal timer is not compiled, requesting
this flag returns an error. The counting thread and its state belong to the
.IR entropy_collector
it was spawned for, so instances do not interfere with one another; this does
not make a single instance safe to use from two threads. See
.B THREAD SAFETY
in
.BR jitterentropy (3).
.TP
.B JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER
This flag can be used to ensure that the internal timer is not used.
If this flag is used together with
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
this is treated as an error and the allocation returns NULL. Also,
in case no startup self test passes on the time source, the allocation
returns NULL instead of falling back to the internal timer.
.TP
.B JENT_FORCE_FIPS
Force full FIPS 140 and SP800-90B compliance irrespective of the
FIPS setting of the underlying operating system.
.TP
.B JENT_NTG1
Enable the BSI AIS 20/31 v3.0 NTG.1 compliant behavior.
.TP
.B JENT_CACHE_ALL
Derive the memory size of the memory access noise source from the summed
size of all data caches rather than from the L1 data cache alone. Without a
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_*
flag, the size is the next power of two above that sum; with the default of
L1 alone it is four times the next power of two above the L1 size.
.TP
.BR JENT_DISABLE_STIR ", " JENT_DISABLE_UNBIAS
Accepted for compatibility and without effect.
.TP
.B JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM
Require the extras of secure memory for the state of the entropy collector.
Secure memory is memory that is zeroized when it is freed, and every build
provides it. Locking it into RAM and excluding it from core dumps are extras
that some platforms add: a memory lock from the operating system, or an
allocation from the secure memory arena of the crypto library the Jitter RNG
was built against. The Jitter RNG always asks for them where they are
offered; this flag decides what happens when that request cannot be granted.
Without it - the default - the collector is created regardless, at the price
of a state that may be written to the swap space, but is still wiped on
release. With the flag the
allocation fails instead, which is what an environment not permitting
locking - a CI system or a restricted container with a too small
.IR RLIMIT_MEMLOCK ,
say - will run into, as will an application that has not configured the
secure memory arena of libgcrypt (GCRYCTL_INIT_SECMEM) or OpenSSL
.RB ( CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init ()),
each of which is process-wide state the library does not establish
itself. libgcrypt's arena is only used once the application has declared its
initialization finished (GCRYCTL_INITIALIZATION_FINISHED), and libgcrypt's
FIPS mode only once it is initialized at all
.RB ( gcry_check_version ());
before, the decision libgcrypt will take is taken from the same inputs: the
environment variable LIBGCRYPT_FORCE_FIPS_MODE, the file
.I /etc/gcrypt/fips_enabled
and, on Linux,
.IR /proc/sys/crypto/fips_enabled .
On a system in FIPS mode, which implies
.BR JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM ,
a collector allocated before libgcrypt's initialization is finished fails.
.IP
The flag is implied by
.B JENT_NTG1
and
.BR JENT_FORCE_FIPS ,
whose compliance claims cover the protection of the collector state, and by
the system's FIPS mode, in which a collector runs as with
.BR JENT_FORCE_FIPS .
It has no effect in the Linux kernel, where kernel memory has the extras by
construction. With libgcrypt and OpenSSL the extras are what their secure
arena provides, and the library adds no memory lock or core dump exclusion of
its own: whether the arena is locked is decided by the crypto library and the
environment it was created in. AWS-LC has no secure arena, and a platform
without a memory lock offers no extras either, so the flag has no effect
there: the memory is wiped on release, and nothing more.
.IP
The memory access region, whose size
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_*
sets, is not part of that state and never gets the memory lock: it is what
the memory access noise source is timed on, and its contents are neither
hashed into the entropy pool nor part of any output. It is zeroed on
allocation and wiped on release, and excluded from core dumps where the
platform allows, but it is not locked and not taken from a secure memory
arena. What a collector
locks is therefore a small fixed amount whatever its memory size: one page of
state, a second with the internal timer, and two more for the delta history
while its startup runs. A health test reallocation holds two collectors while
it runs, and three when the startup of the replacement fails a health test in
turn: the collector in use, the replacement, and the collector that measures
the clock for, or then replaces, that replacement. The small default quotas -
the 64 KiB
.I RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
of older Linux systems and of Android apps, the 200 KiB minimum working set a
Windows process starts with, against which
.BR VirtualLock ()
is charged - hold several collectors at a time; the Windows default held 44.
An application allocating more raises the quotas itself, with
.BR setrlimit ()
or
.BR SetProcessWorkingSetSizeEx ();
.I tests/jitterentropy-memlock.h
does this for the tools shipped with the library and can be used as it stands.
The library does not do it itself: the limits are process-wide state,
extending them evicts what the host application reserved, and they cannot be
restored on free.
.TP
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_*
Set the size of the memory access region, which is then used as given.
Without one of these flags, the size is derived from the data caches: four
times the next power of two above the L1 data cache size, or with
.B JENT_CACHE_ALL
the next power of two above the sum of all data caches. Where no cache size
is discoverable, it is 256 KiB. On 32-bit targets a derived size is capped at
64 MiB. A derived size doubles with every reallocation after a health test
failure; one set by a flag does not.
The larger the memory, the more entropy is collected, yet the default is safe
on most CPUs. With memory pressure but a sufficient entropy rate a smaller
size may be used; with insufficient entropy a larger one.
The flags are
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_1kB " (1)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_2kB " (2)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_4kB " (3)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_8kB " (4)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_16kB " (5)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_32kB " (6)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_64kB " (7)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_128kB " (8)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_256kB " (9)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_512kB " (10)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_1MB " (11)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_2MB " (12)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_4MB " (13)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_8MB " (14)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_16MB " (15)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_32MB " (16)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_64MB " (17)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_128MB " (18)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_256MB " (19)",
and
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_512MB " (20)",
which is also
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_MAX ,
the index in parentheses. The size is 2 to the power of the index plus
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_OFFSET
(9) bytes.
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_TO_FLAGS ()
turns an index into its flag and
.BR JENT_FLAGS_TO_MAX_MEMSIZE ()
back, shifting by
.B JENT_FLAGS_TO_MEMSIZE_SHIFT
(27), and
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_MASK
selects the field in
.IR flags .
.TP
.B JENT_HASHLOOP_*
Set the number of hash loop iterations per measurement:
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_1 " (1)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_2 " (2)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_4 " (3)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_8 " (4)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_16 " (5)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_32 " (6)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_64 " (7)",
and
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_128 " (8)",
which is also
.BR JENT_MAX_HASHLOOP ,
the index in parentheses: index
.I n
selects 2^(\fIn\fR\-1) iterations.
Without one of these flags - index 0 - the built-in default is used, one
iteration unless the build sets a different one with
.BR JENT_HASH_LOOP_DEFAULT .
Every reallocation after a health test failure doubles the count, up to the
maximum.
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_TO_FLAGS ()
turns an index into its flag and
.BR JENT_FLAGS_TO_HASHLOOP ()
back, shifting by
.B JENT_FLAGS_TO_HASHLOOP_SHIFT
(23), and
.B JENT_MAX_HASHLOOP_MASK
selects the field in
.IR flags .
.LP
Bits 9 to 22 of
.I flags
are reserved, as are memory size field values above
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_MAX
and hash loop field values above
.BR JENT_MAX_HASHLOOP .
Flags using them are refused rather than ignored:
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex (3)
returns
.I EPROGERR
and
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
NULL. A flag a later version defines is therefore not silently taken by one
that does not know what it asks for.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
returns the new collector, or NULL on failure.
.BR jent_entropy_collector_free ()
returns no value.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
See
.B THREAD SAFETY
in
.BR jitterentropy (3)
for the details.
.ad l
.TS
allbox;
lbx lb
lx l.
Interface	Thread safety
T{
\%\fBjent_entropy_collector_alloc\fP()
T}	MT-Safe
T{
\%\fBjent_entropy_collector_free\fP()
T}	MT-Safe race:entropy_collector
.TE
.ad
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jent_entropy_init (3),
.BR jent_read_entropy (3),
.BR jent_status (3),
.BR jitterentropy (3),
.BR mlock (2)
